# The stereoscopic depth budget, and the overlay that has to share it.
#
# Two claims, both of which used to be false:
#
#  1. The deviation — the near-to-far separation, as a fraction of the image
#     width — stays inside the 1/30 the stereo literature allows *at every
#     zoom*. The old rule scaled the stereo base with the camera distance, which
#     is the wrong quantity: the board is a fixed-size object, so coming closer
#     shrinks the nearest point in frame far faster than it shrinks the
#     distance. Measured with the shipped configuration, that gave 1/20 of the
#     image width at the default zoom and 1/12 zoomed in. See src/Stereo.h and
#     the articles in res/stereo.zip.
#
#  2. The glyph overlay draws both eyes. It used to draw one, flat, at the
#     screen plane, while the board had parallax — the move numbers and
#     coordinates hovered in front of the wood they are supposed to be lying on.
#
# `overlay_glyphs` counts text items actually drawn, so under an anaglyph shader
# it must exactly double: the same labels, once per eye.

new_game 9
wait_idle
switch_player black human
switch_player white human
wait_idle
wait_until camera_animating false

toggle_coordinates on
click 2 2
click 2 2
wait_until move_count >= 1
click 6 6
click 6 6
wait_until move_count >= 2
wait 300

# Mono to begin with: eighteen coordinate labels on a 9x9, plus the move number
# on the last stone.
expect stereo false
expect overlay_glyphs 19

# The budget is a property of the camera, not of the shader, so it is reported
# and bounded even here.
expect stereo_deviation <= 0.0334

# --- The anaglyph shaders ----------------------------------------------------
# Four steps along the list reaches [stereo] Red Carpet. Asserted rather than
# assumed: if the shader list is ever reordered this says so instead of quietly
# testing the wrong thing.
cycle shaders
cycle shaders
cycle shaders
cycle shaders
wait_idle
expect stereo true

# Every label, once per eye. Nineteen items, thirty-eight draws.
#
# wait_until rather than expect: wait_idle above returns on the frame the shader
# became current, and overlay_glyphs reports what the glyph pass last *drew* — so
# the rebuild into two eyes has been asked for but need not have happened yet.
# Quiescent is not the same as repainted.
wait_until overlay_glyphs 38
expect stereo_deviation <= 0.0334

# Where the scene meets the glass, which the deviation above says nothing about —
# the window cancels in near-minus-far, so it needs its own assertion.
#
# The window rests on the nearest thing in frame: ratio 1 means the near point is
# exactly at the screen plane, so the scene recedes behind the glass and nothing
# comes forward through it. That last part is not only taste — the RmlUi
# interface is drawn flat *at* the screen plane, so anything in negative parallax
# collides with the menus.
#
# A ratio rather than a distance, because this is the invariant: it holds at
# every zoom and aspect, where a distance is camera specific. Asserted again
# after the zoom below, which is what the old fixed window could not survive.
expect stereo_convergence_ratio >= 0.999
expect stereo_convergence_ratio <= 1.001
expect stereo_board_far > 0

# --- The zoom that used to break it ------------------------------------------
# `zoom stones` frames the two stones, which is a much closer camera: the near
# point roughly halves. Under the old rule the deviation would have grown with
# it; under the near-point rule the base shrinks to match and the deviation is
# unchanged.
zoom stones
wait_until camera_animating false
wait 400

expect stereo_near <= 2.0          # the camera really did come in
expect stereo_base <= 0.03         # ...and the base came in with it
expect stereo_deviation <= 0.0334  # which is the whole point

# And the window came in with them — still resting on the near point rather than
# staying put in world units while the board advances through it. A fixed `dof`
# could not do this: it put the near point 3.6% of the image width behind the
# glass at 4:3 and 1.9% at 16:9, values nobody chose.
expect stereo_convergence_ratio >= 0.999
expect stereo_convergence_ratio <= 1.001

# The overlay is still drawing both eyes at the new camera — the base it uses is
# the same value the shader is handed, so there is nothing to drift.
expect overlay_glyphs 38

# --- And back to mono --------------------------------------------------------
# Two more steps returns to the head of the list. The count halves again, which
# is what says the doubling above was the eyes and not something else.
cycle shaders
cycle shaders
wait_idle
expect stereo false
wait_until overlay_glyphs 19
expect stereo_deviation <= 0.0334
